      "content": "To troubleshoot a mounting or permission issue, check the following:\n\n- Run `kubectl logs` to inspect the container's logs for any permission-related errors or Mount errors.\n- Check the volume mounting configuration in your manifest to ensure that the correct volume is being mounted to the `/app/dist/` directory.\n- Ensure that the service account has the necessary permissions to access the mounted volume.\n- Inspect the SecurityContext of the container to confirm that the container is running with the correct UID/GID.\n- Try using a Volume Mount instead of a Volume with a Persistent Disk or read-only file system to rule out permission and permission issues.\n\nAdditionally, you can try to debug your issue by setting `localhost` as the docker networking driver and then using `kubectl port-forward` to expose your pod's port to your local machine. This should allow you to write logs to the local directory and receive the logs.",
